<div class="cvp-wizard-body">
  <div class="cvp-wizard-scroll">

  <div class="cvp-wizard-progress">Step {{stepNumber}} of {{totalSteps}}</div>

  {{#if isIntro}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-intro">
    <h2>Learn D&D 5e's Settings</h2>
    <p>
      dnd5e ships with a lot of rules knobs — some quietly fix a gap between Foundry's defaults and the
      actual rulebook, others are genuine house-rule variants where different tables want different
      answers. None of them have one universally "correct" value.
    </p>
    <p>
      This wizard won't change anything for you. Each step explains what a setting does and gives
      concrete reasons you might want it on, off, or set a different way — then hands you a button
      that opens the real Foundry dialog where you make the call yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if isFoundation}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-setting-step">
    <h2>The Foundation</h2>
    <p>Two settings shape everything else in the ruleset — worth checking first.</p>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Rules Version <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{rulesVersionCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Switches dnd5e between the 2024 ("modern") and 2014 ("legacy") rule sets — spell text, class
        features, and stat blocks all follow this.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> pick Legacy if you're running a
        published 2014-era adventure and want stat blocks and spell text to match the book you're
        reading from, or if your table simply prefers the older rules. Pick Modern for anything written
        for or updated to the 2024 rules, or if you're starting fresh with no legacy material to match.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Grid Diagonals <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{gridDiagonalsCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Controls what a diagonal move costs on the grid. dnd5e's actual rule (both editions) is
        <strong>alternating 5/10/5</strong> — the first diagonal costs 5ft, the second 10ft, alternating
        from there.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> switch to Alternating if you
        want movement and range measurement to strictly match the rulebook — it's not a house rule,
        it's the actual RAW cost. Plenty of tables still run every-diagonal-costs-5ft anyway because
        it's simpler mental math and the difference rarely swings a fight; that's a deliberate,
        reasonable simplification too, not a mistake.</p>
      <button type="button" class="cvp-wizard-btn" data-action="showMe" data-key="settings-tab">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-location-crosshairs"></i> Show me where
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if isCombat}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-setting-step">
    <h2>Combat &amp; Initiative</h2>
    <p>All five of these live in one dialog — <strong>Configure Combat</strong>.</p>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Dexterity Tiebreaker <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{dexTiebreakerCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>RAW breaks initiative ties using the higher raw Dexterity score.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn it on for strict RAW play
        and one less manual ruling at the table. Leave it off if you'd rather adjudicate ties yourself
        — a coin flip, a reroll, or just narrative judgment.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Initiative Score <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{initiativeScoreCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>The alternative to rolling: a flat "10 + initiative bonus" score, used instead of a d20 roll.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> switch to a fixed score for
        faster, more predictable combat starts — useful for organized play or tables that find rolling
        for turn order to be dead time. Keep rolling if initiative uncertainty is part of the excitement
        for your group.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Critical Damage — Multiply Modifiers <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{critMultiplyCurrent}}</span></div>
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Critical Damage — Maximize Dice <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{critMaximizeCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Two independent ways to make crits hit harder than standard RAW (which just doubles the dice).</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn either on if your table
        wants crits to feel more dramatic and swingy, especially at higher levels where flat modifiers
        matter more. Leave both off to match published encounter math exactly — CR and monster HP are
        balanced around standard crit rules.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Recharge Abilities (NPCs) <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{rechargeCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether abilities like "Recharge 5-6" roll automatically at the start of the NPC's turn instead
        of waiting for a manual click.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> automate it to save clicks when
        running several recharge-ability monsters at once. Leave it manual if you like choosing, for
        pacing or drama, exactly when a recharge "happens."</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">NPC Hit Points <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{npcHpCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether a new NPC token rolls its actual hit dice for HP, instead of using the flat average
        printed in the stat block.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> roll for variance and
        unpredictability — no two goblins are quite the same. Keep the average if you want your CR-based
        encounter math to match the book exactly, or you're balancing an encounter in advance.</p>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="cvp-wizard-btn" data-action="openMenu" data-menu="combatConfiguration">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-swords"></i> Open Combat Settings
    </button>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if isCampaignStyle}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-setting-step">
    <h2>Pacing &amp; Campaign Style</h2>
    <p>These live under <strong>Configure Variant Rules</strong> — the ones with the biggest effect on
      how your whole campaign feels.</p>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Rest Variant <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{restVariantCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Standard PHB rests are a 1-hour short rest and an 8-hour long rest. "Gritty Realism" stretches
        that to an 8-hour short rest and a 7-day long rest; "Epic" shrinks it to minutes.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> Gritty Realism if you want
        resource attrition (spell slots, HP) to matter across a whole in-game week — a slower,
        survival-flavored pace. Epic if you want fast, cinematic, high-power one-shots where resources
        barely matter. Standard PHB fits most ordinary campaigns.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Proficiency Variant <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{proficiencyCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>The alternative to a flat proficiency bonus is a scaling proficiency <em>die</em> you roll and
        add, instead of adding a fixed number.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> the dice variant adds extra
        swing and "anything can happen" flavor some tables enjoy. The flat bonus is simpler, more
        predictable, and easier to plan around when building or judging a character.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Leveling Mode <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{levelingModeCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether characters level up by tracked experience points, or whenever the GM decides the story
        has reached that point (Milestone).</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> Milestone removes XP
        bookkeeping entirely and ties pacing to the story instead of combat math. XP tracking gives
        players visible, concrete feedback and rewards proactive engagement with the fiction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Encumbrance Tracking <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{encumbranceCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether carried weight is tracked at all, and what happens when a character exceeds their
        capacity. <span class="cvp-setting-sub">Track Currency Weight: {{currencyWeightCurrent}}
        (only matters if encumbrance is on)</span></p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn it on for
        survival/dungeon-crawl campaigns where "can you actually carry this out" is a real decision.
        Leave it off for narrative-forward campaigns where it's rarely relevant and just adds
        bookkeeping.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Honor / Sanity Ability Scores
        <span class="cvp-setting-current">Honor: {{honorScoreCurrent}} · Sanity: {{sanityScoreCurrent}}</span>
      </div>
      <p>Two optional 7th ability scores from specific published settings — Honor for
        honor-driven settings, Sanity for horror/madness-driven ones.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> only turn one on if your
        campaign is actually built around that theme — otherwise it's an unused stat on every sheet.</p>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="cvp-wizard-btn" data-action="openMenu" data-menu="variantRulesConfiguration">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-list-check"></i> Open Variant Rules Settings
    </button>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if isVisibility}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-setting-step">
    <h2>Secrecy &amp; Visibility</h2>
    <p>All four live under <strong>Configure Visibility</strong> — how much mechanical information
      players see versus what stays hidden behind the GM screen.</p>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Challenge Visibility <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{challengeVisibilityCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Controls whether players see the DCs of checks, and whether success/failure gets highlighted
        in chat.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> show more for a transparent,
        rules-forward table that likes knowing exactly what they needed to hit. Hide more to preserve
        mystery and let the GM narrate outcomes without the table math-checking every roll.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Attack Result Visibility <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{attackVisibilityCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether the attack roll's actual number shows up in the chat card for players.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> show it for faster resolution
        and no "did that hit?" questions. Hide it to keep the GM narrating hit/miss instead of the
        table reading it off the math.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Bloodied Status <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{bloodiedCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Whether a "bloodied" (half HP or less) marker shows on enemy tokens, and to whom.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> show it to everyone for real
        tactical information — players can make informed focus-fire or retreat calls. Restrict it to
        keep how tough a monster still is a genuine unknown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Conceal NPC Descriptions <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{concealDescriptionsCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Hides an NPC or monster item's description text from players when the GM posts it to chat.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn it on if item
        descriptions tend to spoil information the characters haven't actually learned yet — a secret
        weakness or hidden ability written right in the text.</p>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="cvp-wizard-btn" data-action="openMenu" data-menu="visibilityConfiguration">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-eye"></i> Open Visibility Settings
    </button>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if isClosing}}
  <div class="cvp-wizard-step cvp-wizard-closing">
    <h2>Bastions (2024 DMG downtime)</h2>
    <p>
      One more corner worth knowing about: <strong>Bastions</strong> is the 2024 DMG's downtime/stronghold
      subsystem, letting level-5+ characters build facilities and issue orders that resolve over time.
      It's entirely optional — these settings only matter if your campaign actually uses it.
    </p>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Loyalty Score <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{loyaltyScoreCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>A separate toggle (not part of the Bastion dialog below) that enables optional Loyalty
        tracking for NPCs — hirelings and followers get a trackable loyalty stat.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn it on if you're running
        Bastion facilities staffed by hirelings and want their loyalty to matter mechanically. Leave it
        off if you're not using Bastions, or want a simpler downtime experience without an extra stat to
        track per NPC.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Enable Bastion Functionality <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{bastionEnabledCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Gives level-5+ characters a Bastion tab on their sheet, where they build facilities and issue
        orders to them.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn on if your campaign
        includes the Bastion subsystem at all. Leave off if you're not running it — it adds a fair
        amount of new UI and bookkeeping to every eligible character sheet, so there's no reason to turn
        it on until you actually intend to use it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Show Bastion Turn Button <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{bastionTurnButtonCurrent}}</span></div>
      <p>Adds a button to the main interface for manually advancing Bastion turns — the time unit
        Bastion orders resolve on.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> turn on once Bastions are
        enabled and you want a quick, one-click way to advance turns for the whole table, instead of
        doing it from inside each character's Bastion tab.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cvp-setting-card">
      <div class="cvp-setting-name">Bastion Turn Duration <span class="cvp-setting-current">{{bastionTurnDurationCurrent}} days</span></div>
      <p>How many in-game days pass per Bastion turn — the pacing rate at which facility orders
        resolve.</p>
      <p class="cvp-setting-reason"><strong>Reasons to change it:</strong> shorten it for a snappier
        downtime loop where facility orders resolve quickly. Lengthen it if you want Bastion progress to
        feel like a slower, longer-term investment that better matches a lower-frequency session
        cadence.</p>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="cvp-wizard-btn" data-action="openMenu" data-menu="bastionConfiguration">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-chess-rook"></i> Open Bastion Settings
    </button>

    <p>Nothing was changed while you read this — every value in this World is exactly what it was
      before you opened the wizard. You can reopen this wizard anytime from <code>/help</code>.</p>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  </div>

  <div class="cvp-wizard-nav">
    {{#if canBack}}
    <button type="button" data-action="back" class="cvp-wizard-btn">Back</button>
    {{/if}}
    {{#unless isClosing}}
    <button type="button" data-action="next" class="cvp-wizard-btn cvp-wizard-btn--primary">
      {{#if isIntro}}Get Started{{else}}Next{{/if}}
    </button>
    {{/unless}}
  </div>

</div>
